    <!-- Cluster -->
    <section id="cluster" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">The Cluster Effect: Why Anping Costs Less to Begin With</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Anping County, about 250 km south of Beijing, has made wire mesh since the 1950s—originally for the local silk-weaving trade to convert its looms to metal. Seven decades later the cluster includes 500+ registered manufacturers and several thousand supporting workshops, and the density itself is the cost advantage:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Shared labor pool.</strong> Welders, loom technicians, and galvanizing crews move between factories. Skills are universal and wages—while rising—remain a fraction of Western levels. Nobody trains anyone; everyone hires ready specialists.</li>
        <li><strong>Wire rod on the doorstep.</strong> Hebei is China's steel heartland. Q195/Q235 low-carbon rod arrives from Shijiazhuang and Handan mills by truck in hours, often on consignment, so factories carry almost no raw-material inventory cost.</li>
        <li><strong>Outsourced everything.</strong> Galvanizing, PVC coating, crimping, edge finishing, packing, and even export documentation are available as services within the county. A "factory" can be a welding shop plus three subcontractors and still deliver a complete, certified product.</li>
        <li><strong>Logistics scale.</strong> Container stuffing yards, forwarders, and customs brokers compete within one county. A full 20 ft container of mesh can be booked, stuffed, and exported in days at rock-bottom local rates.</li>
        <li><strong>Machine building locally.</strong> Welding lines and weaving looms are manufactured in the cluster itself, so capital equipment is cheap and maintenance is instant—an unusual self-sufficiency that keeps depreciation costs low.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        The result: a factory in Anping producing <a href="/products/welded-wire-mesh/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">welded wire mesh</a> operates with structural costs 40–60% below a comparable Western or even coastal-Chinese plant. None of this is a trick. It is what industrial clustering does everywhere—it just happens to be extreme here because the entire world's mesh demand funnels into one county.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Neijuan -->
    <section id="neijuan" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">内卷 Explained: Overcapacity and the Real Margin Math</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        The Chinese term 内卷 (neijuan, "involution") describes competition so intense that everyone works harder for the same or shrinking returns—and Anping is a textbook case. After two decades of export growth, the cluster built more capacity than the world needs. Wire mesh production lines there are estimated to run at 60–75% utilization, and every factory fights for orders against a neighbor making an identical product with identical machines.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        The arithmetic for a typical galvanized welded mesh order looks roughly like this:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
