              <td class="p-3">5–10%</td>
              <td class="p-3">Spread over high output</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Factory margin</td>
              <td class="p-3">3–8%</td>
              <td class="p-3">Where neijuan bites—this is what competition fights over</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Two lessons follow. First, <strong>genuine savings for buyers come from the margin line</strong>: in a normal year factories gladly sacrifice half their margin to fill a line, which is why quotes from five Anping suppliers can vary 8–15% for identical specs and all be legitimate. Second, <strong>nobody sells below steel cost for long</strong>. A quote below the cluster's raw-material floor is not a generous factory—it is a factory planning to ship you less steel or less zinc than specified. The competitive structure that gives you honest cheap mesh is the same structure that rewards the corner-cutters; the buyer's job is telling them apart.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">What This Means for Your Negotiation</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          With 3–5 comparable quotes, the cheapest legitimate quote is usually 5–10% below the median. Pressing further rarely unlocks real savings—it changes what you receive. Negotiate payment terms, packaging, and delivery instead; those have slack. Price below the raw-material floor has no slack at all.
        </p>
      </div>
