    <!-- Tricks -->
    <section id="tricks" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">The Three Corner-Cutting Tricks</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Nearly every "too cheap to be true" shipment we have audited fails in one of three ways. Each saves the factory real money while leaving the product looking almost right:
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-4 mb-6">
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Trick 1: Thin Wire</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm mb-2">
            Wire diameter tolerance under ASTM A641/A974 is ±5%, and a factory targeting the bottom of that band is within its rights. The cheat goes further: running 0.02–0.05 mm below nominal. On a 10-ton order of 4.0 mm wire, shipping 3.90 mm saves roughly 5% of the steel cost—$1,500–2,500 at current rod prices. The mesh looks identical, welds fine, and fails your load test months later. Mesh count can be padded the same way: one extra wire per 25 mm adds apparent density while consuming aperture you specified.
          </p>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            <strong>Catch it:</strong> micrometer at 5 points per roll, and a scale. Weight never lies.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Trick 2: Light Zinc</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm mb-2">
            Zinc is the most expensive input after steel, and coating weight is invisible to the eye. Shipping electro-galvanized (15–25 g/m²) as "galvanized," or hot-dip at 170 g/m² against a quoted 275 g/m², saves 2–4% of order value while halving outdoor life. The giveaway used to be appearance—thin zinc looks bright and smooth—but modern lines can make light coatings look spangled and heavy, so eyes are no longer evidence.
          </p>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            <strong>Catch it:</strong> magnetic coating gauge on weld points and flat wire, or a strip test (ASTM A90) on a sample. Write the test method into the contract so an inspector can enforce it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Trick 3: Narrow Width and Short Length</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm mb-2">
            A roll sold as 2.0 m wide arrives at 1.95 m; a 30 m roll measures 29 m. Width tolerance on rolls is commonly ±10 mm, but the cheat exploits that the buyer almost never unrolls and measures a full roll. Across a container of 500 rolls, a 5 cm width shortfall is 25 m² of missing mesh—pure profit. Panels get the same treatment at the shear: 2.44 m cut to 2.40 m and quietly shrugged off as "tolerance."
          </p>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            <strong>Catch it:</strong> measure three rolls fully out of every container (or have your inspector do it), and check the total square meters delivered against the packing list to two decimal places.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Note what these tricks share: they all reduce <em>mass</em>. That is why a scale plus a calculator defeats all three at once.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Catch -->
    <section id="catch" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">How to Catch Every Trick With Calculators</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        You do not need a lab. The verification stack is: one scale, one micrometer, and two free tools—run in this order:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Step 1 — before ordering:</strong> enter the quoted spec into the <a href="/tools/welded-wire-mesh-weight-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">welded mesh weight calculator</a> (or the <a href="/tools/woven-wire-mesh-weight-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">woven mesh calculator</a> for filtration mesh). Record the theoretical kg/m². If the factory's quoted weight per roll is more than ~3% below theory, ask why—in writing.</li>
        <li><strong>Step 2 — on samples:</strong> weigh the sample, measure wire diameter and width, recompute. A consistent 5–8% shortfall means the quote was engineered around thinner wire; walk away now.</li>
        <li><strong>Step 3 — at pre-shipment inspection:</strong> have the inspector weigh 3 random rolls, micrometer-check wire, gauge the zinc, and measure width/length. Compare against the Step 1 numbers. Cost: $150–300; value: your balance payment.</li>
        <li><strong>Step 4 — on the quote itself:</strong> strip any CIF quote to FOB with the <a href="/tools/fob-price-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">FOB price calculator</a> so freight padding cannot disguise a suspicious product price—or make a thin-wire quote look normal by inflating freight.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Red Flag</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Likely Trick</th>
