    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Is all cheap wire mesh from China low quality?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            No. Low price in Anping usually reflects the cluster's genuine cost advantages—shared labor, local steel, outsourced services, and thin margins from overcapacity. Many cluster factories also produce premium export grade with full certification. Price tells you nothing by itself; verification (weight, coating, dimensions) tells you everything.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How much cheaper is Anping wire mesh compared to buying locally?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Typical landed-cost savings are 30–50% versus buying from Western distributors for standard galvanized welded mesh and fence products, less for stainless woven mesh where alloy price dominates. The gap narrows on small orders (under ~$3,000) because freight and fixed fees eat the advantage—see our <a href="/blog/how-to-import-wire-mesh-from-china/" class="text-theme-primary underline">import guide</a> for the break-even math.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What is 内卷 (neijuan) and why should a buyer care?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Neijuan—often translated as "involution"—is competition that intensifies without growing the market: 500 factories chasing the same export orders. Buyers benefit through margin compression (factories quote 5–10% below cost-plus to fill lines) but face the flip side: factories under extreme price pressure may quietly shave wire diameter, zinc weight, or roll dimensions to survive. The tools and checks above let you take the benefit without accepting the risk.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Can I trust a factory that offers prices well below everyone else?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Treat it as a hypothesis to test, not an opportunity. Ask for the arithmetic behind the price (current rod and zinc costs), demand the weight per roll in writing, and order samples before any deposit. If the sample weighs within ~3% of the calculator's theoretical weight and coating/width check out, the low price may simply be a hungry factory. If the sample is light, the quote was bait.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Does the cheapest quote ever win legitimately?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Yes—when quotes are within the honest band, the cheapest is often a factory with slack capacity this month, and the product will be identical because every cluster factory buys the same rod from the same mills. The discipline is in the word "band": normalize every quote with the <a href="/tools/fob-price-calculator/" class="text-theme-primary underline">FOB price calculator</a>, discard outliers below the raw-material floor, and choose among the survivors on verification results and communication quality, not price alone.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

